US president has declared a national  emergency over the corona virus pandemic  as the number of cases raises across the  country Donald Trump described the next  eight weeks as critical to fighting the  respiratory illness he said half a  million coronavirus tests will be  available as early as next week and  urged every state to set up emergency  centers to treat patients  to unleash the full power of the federal  government in this effort today I am  officially declaring a national  emergency two very big words the action  I am taking will open up access to up to  fifty billion dollars of very  importantly very important and a large  amount of money for states and  territories and localities and our  shared fight against this disease in  furtherance of the order emerging every  state to set up emergency operation  centers effective immediately I'm also  asking every hospital in this country to  activate its emergency preparedness plan  so that they can meet the needs of  Americans everywhere so what does this  practically mean now on the ground and  for Americans Kimberly well what it  means is that there's going to be a lot  more money now to blunt the outbreak  here in the United States we now have  more than 1,900 cases of corona virus in  at least 46 states with at least 41  deaths so there is a real interest of  public health officials who spoke in the  Rose Garden alongside the US president  to try and combat this outbreak and so  they're doing it in a number of ways not  only by declaring a national emergency  which frees up tens of billions for the  federal government to coordinate with  the state and local governments but also  we saw the president surrounded by a  number of key industry officials from  the private sector and what the  president has done has kind of taken his  transactional approach to government  which we've seen in the past and this is  no exception in bringing in industry  leaders from from private companies like  Google for example even Walmart setting  up a website so that Americans can  easily on a smartphone access whether or  not a they need a test and B if they do  where to get it setting up in parking  lots around the United States different  areas where people can go and get this  test in a very American style in their  cars they'll be drive through type  swabbing that will be taking place so  this is something that the president is  saying will take place  by the end of this weekend the in terms  of the website being set up with the  tasks some five hundred thousand of them  by the middle of the week now he also  said that there may be up to five  million tests ultimately available  saying and dismissing don't think we'll  need that many that could be something  that could by the president later on but  for now he is continuing to take an  optimistic and upbeat tone yeah and  specifically on that tone Kimberly what  do we make of that because the president  has faced criticism from some people  saying that he's been slow and  ineffective in his response to the  crisis so far yeah there's no question  about it in fact and I don't want to  sort of get ahead of my colleague  Kristen salumi I know you we talked to  you with the financial markets but  certainly was a key factor that led to a  lot of anxiety in this country all  across the board the fact that there  seemed to be this feeling that the  president may have been even suppressing  the availability of tasks through his  government in order to artificially keep  numbers down of the outbreak in terms of  coronavirus here in the United States  now he was asked and that Rose Garden  press conference do you take  responsibility for that fact he said  just the opposite and don't take  responsibility for the lag in testing in  fact he tried to blame the previous  administration under Barack Obama saying  it was an outdated system not equipped  for this type of a pandemic they've  quickly adapted the system getting rid  of a lot of regulations allowing for  private labs to be involved in  coordination with the federal government  in order to try and combat this moving  forward saying they'll be set up for  success in the future I think it is a  message that at least for now until the  Americans see whether or not it actually  works I think many will find reassuring  all right Kimberly thank you for that  update from Washington well you heard  Kimberly mentioning markets a moment ago  and they did rally during President  Trump's announcement the Dow Jones  Industrial Average closed up 9.3% after  trading around three percent up for most  of the day Thursday saw the greatest  losses since the Black Monday crash of  1987 in London the footsie 100 initially  surged 8% but gains subsided and  afternoon trading to close just two  point four percent up it's tough  it's biggest weekly loss in more than 30  years and in Asia markets plunged and  warning trade but managed to call back  initial losses in Hong Kong the Hang  Seng Index closed down 1.1 percent let's  get an update from Krishna salumi  joining us from New York so how did the  markets react to what President Trump  had to say it was really interesting  during this press conference was timed  shortly before the markets closed less  than an hour before markets closed  President Trump made his remarks pushing  all three of the major stock indices up  over 9% regaining most of what they lost  on Thursday I think there were a couple  things that the president did and said  that investors found particularly  reassuring the first was talking about  increasing testing capacity in the  United States that was a big concern  across the board and in this country and  with investors in particular and he did  this surrounded by not only health  officials but corporate CEOs  representing companies like Walmart in  Google and Walgreens talking about what  was being done to increase testing  capacity and showing that businesses are  going to be part of that solution I  think that's something that investors in  particular will find very reassuring he  also announced that the Energy  Department would be buying up strategic  would be buying up oil to top off the  strategic oil reserves for the country  that was also something that the markets  found very reassuring oil prices which  had been plummeting went up a bit today  energy stocks which also had been  suffering as a result of lower demand  and a price war between the Russians and  the Saudis they went up today as well so  there was good news there for investors  sharp contrast to earlier in the week  when the president addressed the nation  and announced a travel ban restricted  travel from Europe to the United States  that sent the markets tumbling also he  announced that there would be a relief  package but it was very vague not a lot  of details they did not find that  reassuring at all so the president took  a more  and a specific tone and his remarks  today surrounding himself with all of  those professionals and has seemed to  work you know it's still not clear  whether or not Congress and the White  House will be able to get together and  what they're going to do in terms of a  relief package per se what will be  passed but for now  definitely calming fears the volatility  index for the markets which shows just  how jittery investors are has been  sky-high in the last week and it's down  at the close of the markets today down  25% so that's a sign that at least for  now  investors are breathing a sigh of relief  alright so Krishna salumi thank you
 

